Fixed in `tokio-stream/src/stream_map.rs:686`:

```rust
let mut ret: (usize, Option<usize>) = (0, Some(0));

for (_, stream) in &self.entries {
    let hint = stream.size_hint();

    ret.0 = ret.0.saturating_add(hint.0);

    match (ret.1, hint.1) {
        (Some(a), Some(b)) => ret.1 = a.checked_add(b),
        (Some(_), None) => ret.1 = None,
        _ => {}
    }
}
```

- The lower bound saturates at `usize::MAX` instead of overflowing.
- The upper bound becomes `None` (unknown/unbounded) when the sum would overflow, which is the correct conservative report — `checked_add` returning `None` assigns straight into `ret.1`.
- The explicit type annotation is needed because the literal `0`s would otherwise stay an ambiguous `{integer}` and reject the method calls.

This matches how the rest of tokio-stream handles it (`stream_ext.rs:1220`, `stream_close.rs:88`). The full `tokio-stream` suite passes, including `size_hint_overflow` (clippy isn't installed in this environment, so that wasn't run).
